1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:09,159
Many types of hazardous materials are used for the production of energy in industrial processes, the treatment of illness, and for national defense.

2
00:00:10,019 --> 00:00:15,919
The manufacture and use of these materials results in toxic waste which must be disposed of properly.

3
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:21,920
Some of the waste produced by the U.S. Department of Energy's research and development programs is radioactive.

4
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:27,659
One of the disposal areas for this radioactive waste is the Nevada test site.

5
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:45,999
Located 65 miles northwest of Las Vegas, the Nevada test site is the location where the

6
00:00:46,099 --> 00:00:51,720
Department of Energy conducts underground nuclear weapons tests.

7
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:56,260
Radioactive wastes resulting from the nuclear weapons testing program have been disposed

8
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:59,980
of at the site for more than 20 years.

9
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:06,980
waste from other DOE defense program facilities are also taken to the site for disposal.

10
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:14,660
The Nevada Test Site is a remote secure area of 1,350 square miles where experiments involving

11
00:01:15,159 --> 00:01:20,600
hazardous materials can be carried out safely.

12
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:24,919
Radioactive waste management programs at the Nevada Test Site are part of a nationwide

13
00:01:25,019 --> 00:01:31,279
program conducted by the Department of Energy's Office of Nuclear Waste Management, headquartered

14
00:01:31,379 --> 00:01:38,339
in Washington, D.C. The programs include both normal day-to-day waste disposal operations

15
00:01:38,439 --> 00:01:44,339
and research and development projects on new and improved methods for safely handling and

16
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:49,739
disposing of radioactive materials produced by both government and commercial nuclear

17
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:55,680
operations. Every plant and animal that has ever lived on this Earth has been exposed

18
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:01,280
to nuclear radiation for every second of its life by radioactive materials, which occur

19
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:09,460
naturally in our environment. In addition to this natural background radiation, we are

20
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:15,440
also exposed to radiation from medical diagnosis, nuclear weapons test fallout, and energy production

21
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:23,199
and use. There are 106 chemical elements which cannot be divided into simpler substances

22
00:02:23,299 --> 00:02:28,900
by chemical means. Each of these elements may have up to several radioactive isotopes

23
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,900
which have half-lives ranging from millions of a second to millions of years. A half-life

24
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,120
is the time in which half the atoms of a particular radioactive substance disintegrate to another

25
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:46,500
nuclear form. The main concern is that radioactive waste materials would longer have lives be

26
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:52,860
safely disposed of so that they will not be hazardous to people or the environment.

27
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:58,320
Two terms are important in this discussion. Transuranic elements are those elements having

28
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:04,840
an atomic number greater than 92, that of uranium. All transuranic elements are produced

29
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:10,460
artificially and are radioactive. A curie is the basic measurement of the amount of

30
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:16,920
activity of a specific quantity of radioactive material and is based on the rate of decay

31
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:22,940
of one gram of radium. The major classes of nuclear waste involved in programs at the

32
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:30,620
Nevada Test Site are low-level waste, transuranic waste, and high-level waste. Low-level wastes

33
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:35,720
contain less than 10 nanocuries, that is less than ten billionths of a curie of

34
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:41,000
transuranic contaminants per gram of material. Some low-level waste may be

35
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:46,220
free of transuranic contaminants. In general, low-level wastes have shorter

36
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:51,500
half-lives, require minimal shielding, and have small but potentially hazardous

37
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:57,200
quantities of radioactive isotopes. Some low-level waste may have much higher

38
00:03:57,299 --> 00:04:02,199
levels of radioactivity and require heavily shielded, specially designed

39
00:04:02,299 --> 00:04:07,139
transportation containers. Several types of containers are used to transport

40
00:04:07,239 --> 00:04:13,019
radioactive materials. Waste with a small amount of radioactivity may be packaged

41
00:04:13,119 --> 00:04:18,559
in heavy cardboard or plywood boxes. Other higher radioactive substances

42
00:04:18,659 --> 00:04:24,439
require much sturdier containers. All containers must conform to Department of

43
00:04:24,539 --> 00:04:27,140
Transportation regulations.

44
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:33,760
Low-level waste disposed of at the Nevada test site originates in a number of Department

45
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:39,440
of Energy facilities, such as the Rocky Flats plant near Denver, Colorado, where plutonium

46
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:42,920
parts for nuclear weapons are produced.

47
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:47,940
Depending on the type and amount of radioactive material involved, the waste is carefully

48
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:51,080
packed in an appropriate shipping container.

49
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:56,600
These containers must conform to U.S. Department of Transportation regulations and be properly

50
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:03,600
marked and labeled. The content of each container is documented and a detailed inventory is

51
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:16,040
kept on all radioactive material in stock and being shipped to the test site.

52
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:21,619
Each step in the operation, gathering the waste material, consolidating it and packaging

53
00:05:21,719 --> 00:05:27,340
it is carefully planned and checked to ensure that exposure to the plant employees, truck

54
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:33,619
drivers, and the public is kept at the lowest level possible, well below the existing radiation

55
00:05:33,719 --> 00:05:41,040
exposure guidelines.

56
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:45,919
The waste materials destined for the Nevada test site are carried by commercial trucking

57
00:05:46,019 --> 00:05:50,999
firms under regulations established by the U.S. Department of Transportation and the

58
00:05:51,099 --> 00:06:05,600
states through which the trucks travel.

59
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:09,819
Radioactive waste materials also result from a number of activities at the Nevada test

60
00:06:09,919 --> 00:06:10,819
site.

61
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:16,839
Some of the wastes are vehicles, equipment, and debris contaminated by atmospheric nuclear

62
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:25,020
test conducted in the 1950s.

63
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:30,460
This material is being gathered and placed in two large subsidence craters in Yucca Flat

64
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:33,780
formed by underground of Lear tests.

65
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:44,359
This debris will be covered by several feet of earth and the location marked with monuments.

66
00:06:44,459 --> 00:06:49,499
Other low level wastes are generated in test site laboratories and facilities such as the

67
00:06:49,599 --> 00:06:54,699
decontamination pad where equipment used in the underground testing program is cleaned

68
00:06:54,799 --> 00:06:56,259
for reuse.

69
00:06:56,359 --> 00:07:01,480
Rags, protective clothing, and worn out or broken equipment are typical of the waste

70
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:06,460
items that are designated for disposal.

71
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:11,299
These waste materials are compacted and packaged for burial.

72
00:07:11,399 --> 00:07:16,739
And inventory and continuous administrative control is kept of all radioactive materials

73
00:07:16,839 --> 00:07:20,739
on the test site.

74
00:07:20,839 --> 00:07:26,520
The major low-level disposal area at the Nevada test site is a large continuous trench located

75
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:31,780
in Frenchman Flat, a dry lake bed in the southeast section of the site.

76
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:39,919
The trench is 20 feet deep, 700 feet long, and has a capacity for the burial of 1,600,000

77
00:07:40,019 --> 00:07:48,140
cubic feet of packaged radioactive waste. Additional trenches will be dug when needed.

78
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:53,119
As the waste containers are stacked on one side of the trench and covered over, the opposite

79
00:07:53,219 --> 00:07:58,020
side of the trench is extended with the dirt taken to the fill side to be used for future

80
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:06,139
covering. The stacked waste is covered with at least seven feet of dirt.

81
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:11,280
When a truck arrives at the site, the load is checked by a radiation monitor and the

82
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:16,240
containers are unloaded and stacked neatly in place on the side of the trench.

83
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:23,780
The exact location of each box along with a record of its contents is carefully documented.

84
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:33,680
Although highly unlikely, if it is ever necessary to retrieve a container, the record can be

85
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:39,620
checked and the box located then dug out of the trench.

86
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:44,320
Strict controls are required on the material shipped to the test site for burial to ensure

87
00:08:44,420 --> 00:08:47,720
that all Department of Transportation requirements are met.

88
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:53,239
The contents must not include liquids, pyrophoric or explosive materials, combustible material

89
00:08:53,339 --> 00:08:57,720
must be separated from non-combustible and plainly marked.

90
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:02,980
Any heat produced by the contents of the container must not adversely affect the chemical or

91
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:08,340
physical integrity of the contents or the package. The exterior of the package must

92
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:14,340
be free of radioactive contamination upon arrival at the test site. Penetrating gamma

93
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:19,600
radiation must not exceed established guidelines during handling, shipment, and retrievable

94
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:28,619
storage. Packages with waste containing tritium, a radioactive isotope of hydrogen, are overpacked

95
00:09:28,719 --> 00:09:34,399
in steel cylinders. The tritium package is surrounded with a special absorbent material

96
00:09:34,499 --> 00:09:40,159
and the steel container is welded shut prior to burial. Sensitive monitoring equipment

97
00:09:40,259 --> 00:09:46,659
is used to check for leaks of tritium both before and after the containers are buried.

98
00:09:46,759 --> 00:09:51,779
Several research programs on low-level waste currently being conducted include studies

99
00:09:51,879 --> 00:09:57,159
on revegetation to limit the penetration of moisture into the soil, a study on the transport

100
00:09:57,260 --> 00:10:02,860
mechanism of radioactivity through the soil and a study to develop criteria for deeper

101
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,440
burial of waste.

102
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:11,920
The burial site in Frenchman Flat is continuously monitored by air samplers and other sensitive

103
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:14,460
radiation detection equipment.

104
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:19,680
All employees at the Nevada test site wear radiation film disseminators, which are checked

105
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:24,280
monthly to determine the amount of external radiation exposure that the individual may

106
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:29,940
have received. Exposure to individuals is kept at the lowest level possible, well below

107
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:35,420
guidelines. Transuranic waste results predominantly from

108
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:41,480
reprocessing of spent fuel from nuclear reactors and the fabrication of plutonium components

109
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:47,460
used to produce nuclear weapons. Transuranic waste is currently defined as containing more

110
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:54,920
than 10 nanocuries of transuranic elements per gram of material and may contain uranium.

111
00:10:55,020 --> 00:11:00,499
All materials containing more than 10 nanocuries of transuranic isotopes are kept in special

112
00:11:00,599 --> 00:11:06,119
steel surface containers for 20 years or longer so that the material may be retrieved for

113
00:11:06,219 --> 00:11:13,400
reprocessing or permanent disposal.

114
00:11:13,499 --> 00:11:19,100
The radiation burial site in Frenchman Flat is particularly suited to this operation.

115
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:22,079
It lies in a closed basin.

116
00:11:22,179 --> 00:11:28,579
Surface runoff from the infrequent rains flows into the valley where it eventually evaporates.

117
00:11:28,679 --> 00:11:34,679
The local aquifer is about 600 feet deep and there is no known transport of water from

118
00:11:34,779 --> 00:11:37,439
the surface to the aquifer.

119
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:43,440
in the area are monitored regularly to assure that the water is safe to use.

120
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:54,440
Research utilizing high-level waste is conducted under the Nevada Nuclear Waste Storage Investigations Program,

121
00:11:54,540 --> 00:12:01,440
which involves developing technology for handling, packaging, and storing highly radioactive spent fuel assemblies

122
00:12:01,540 --> 00:12:05,440
in different geologic mediums.

123
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:11,440
The Nevada program is part of the national waste management program conducted by the Department of Energy

124
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:18,440
through the Office of Nuclear Waste Isolation, operated by Battelle Memorial Institute at Columbus, Ohio,

125
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:24,440
and at other DOE field offices in Savannah River, South Carolina, and Hanford, Washington.

126
00:12:24,540 --> 00:12:27,440
High level waste may come from two sources.

127
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:32,440
They may be intact fuel assemblies that are being discarded after having served their useful life

128
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:35,999
in a nuclear reactor and are called spent fuel.

129
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,980
High-level waste also results from the reprocessing of the spent fuel

130
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:46,359
to extract the remaining uranium and plutonium for continued use as reactor fuel.

131
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:51,999
These high-level wastes are being considered for disposal in geologic repositories

132
00:12:52,099 --> 00:12:58,540
or by other technical options designed to provide long-term isolation of the wastes from the biosphere.

133
00:12:59,380 --> 00:13:02,860
The spent fuel assemblies being used at the Nevada test site

134
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:06,420
are from the Florida Power and Light Company's Turkey Point reactor.

135
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:12,140
Additional spent fuel assemblies may be obtained from other reactors for use in new programs.

136
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:18,120
A nuclear power reactor uses about 200 fuel assemblies in its reactor core,

137
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:20,680
producing steam to run electric turbines.

138
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:25,080
About one-third of a nuclear reactor's fuel assemblies in the core

139
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:28,040
must be removed and replaced every 18 months.

140
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:34,080
The spent fuel assemblies are highly radioactive and are stored in water pools near the reactor

141
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:39,200
for several years to allow short half-lived radioisotopes to decay.

142
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:44,720
A 25-ton truck-mounted transportation cask is used to move the spent fuel assemblies

143
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:48,340
from the reactor to the Nevada test site.

144
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:54,960
This cask is placed in the reactor water storage pool for the loading of the spent fuel assembly.

145
00:13:55,060 --> 00:13:59,999
Each fuel assembly is about 13 feet in length and 8 1⁄2 inches square.

146
00:14:00,099 --> 00:14:04,379
It contains 200 hollow rods filled with uranium pellets.

147
00:14:04,479 --> 00:14:09,499
The spent fuel assembly is removed from the storage rack and placed in the shipping cask,

148
00:14:09,599 --> 00:14:11,420
which is then sealed.

149
00:14:11,520 --> 00:14:17,300
Each step of the operation is closely checked by radiation monitors to ensure that the operators

150
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:21,900
do not receive an exposure above established guidelines.

151
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:28,020
When the loaded cask is removed from the water storage tank, it is drained, washed and steamed

152
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,020
clean.

153
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:34,460
The cask is then monitored to assure compliance with the Department of Transportation regulations

154
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:38,660
for external radiation and surface contamination.

155
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:43,420
All of the bolts and seals are checked to assure that there are no leaks.

156
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:48,620
The casks used to transport the spent fuel assemblies are designed to withstand accidents

157
00:14:48,719 --> 00:14:54,440
much more severe than would be expected in a normal transportation situation.

158
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:59,600
The certification tests of the transportation casks include having a trailer carrying a

159
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:09,519
cask rammed by a railroad locomotive going 80 miles per hour.

160
00:15:09,619 --> 00:15:15,739
This test showed only superficial damage to the cask without compromising its integrity.

161
00:15:15,839 --> 00:15:21,060
These casks are one of the safest containers for hazardous material used in interstate

162
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:27,279
commerce.

163
00:15:27,379 --> 00:15:32,259
Several of the spent fuel assemblies were first taken to the Battelle Memorial Institute

164
00:15:32,359 --> 00:15:37,639
laboratory in Columbus, Ohio, where they were closely examined in a hot cell, which protected

165
00:15:37,739 --> 00:15:41,019
the operators from exposure to radiation.

166
00:15:41,119 --> 00:15:49,980
The condition of each assembly was carefully documented.

167
00:15:50,080 --> 00:15:55,319
Fuel handling operations at the Nevada test site are conducted at the Encapsulation, Maintenance

168
00:15:55,419 --> 00:16:01,799
and Disassembly, or EMAD facility located on the southwest corner of the test site.

169
00:16:01,899 --> 00:16:07,239
This building was originally designed and constructed to assemble and disassemble nuclear

170
00:16:07,339 --> 00:16:08,559
rocket engines.

171
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:15,140
When a truck containing the spent fuel assemblies arrives at the Nevada test site, the cask

172
00:16:15,240 --> 00:16:20,200
and trailer are carefully checked for surface contamination or leaks.

173
00:16:20,300 --> 00:16:27,859
In the hot bay of the EMAD facility, the cask is unloaded and again closely checked.

174
00:16:27,959 --> 00:16:32,280
This hot bay is one of the largest facilities of its kind in the world, with dense concrete

175
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:36,100
walls and lead glass windows up to 6 feet thick.

176
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:41,080
All of the operations involving highly radioactive materials in the hot bay are performed by

177
00:16:41,180 --> 00:16:45,980
remote control using overhead cranes and sidewall manipulators.

178
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:50,560
Each step of the operation is observed and recorded by closed circuit television cameras

179
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:53,980
and video tape recorders.

180
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:58,600
Following removal from the transportation cask, the spent fuel assembly is placed in

181
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:03,779
a stainless steel canister.

182
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:08,480
A threaded lid is screwed into the top of the canister and welded closed using specially

183
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:12,640
remotely operated equipment designed for this operation.

184
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:17,280
The sealed canister is filled with helium to provide a chemically inert environment,

185
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:22,739
then is checked for any leaks and surface contamination.

186
00:17:22,839 --> 00:17:27,860
Two methods of temporary surface and near-surface storage are being evaluated at the Nevada

187
00:17:27,960 --> 00:17:28,860
test site.

188
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:33,780
If adopted, one or more of these methods could be used to store high-level radioactive waste

189
00:17:33,879 --> 00:17:39,779
materials prior to final disposal or to provide overflow storage capacity for spent fuel.

190
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:46,420
For the surface storage, an above-ground sealed silo has been designed to virtually eliminate

191
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:52,499
external radiation and withstand a tip-over caused by an earthquake without significant damage.

192
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:58,499
The spent fuel assembly is loaded into the silo in the hot bay and then trucked to a pad located

193
00:17:58,599 --> 00:18:02,380
beside the EMAD building, where it is bolted into place.

194
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:05,999
The surface storage cask is made of steel reinforced concrete.

195
00:18:06,099 --> 00:18:11,440
It is 21 feet high, 9 feet in diameter, and weighs about 95 tons.

196
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:17,279
It sits on a reinforced concrete pad 14 feet square and 6 feet thick.

197
00:18:17,379 --> 00:18:23,940
For the near surface storage, five dry wells, 25 feet deep and 18 inches in diameter, have

198
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:27,700
been drilled between railroad tracks near the EMAD.

199
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:33,499
The dry wells are 25 feet apart and are equipped with steel liners that have been cemented

200
00:18:33,599 --> 00:18:34,660
into place.

201
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:40,160
A specially designed shielded vehicle mounted on a rail car transports the encapsulated

202
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:44,900
fuel assemblies and emplaces them in the dry wells.

203
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:50,660
Both the dry wells and surface storage casks are instrumented to collect and record canister

204
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:57,680
and liner temperatures, soil temperatures near the wells and radiation levels.

205
00:18:57,780 --> 00:19:09,460
Once the fuel canisters are in place, workers can move around both the surface storage cask and the dry wells with virtually no exposure to the radiation from the emplaced spent fuel assembly.

206
00:19:10,599 --> 00:19:20,120
A second experimental program involves the use of 11 canisters containing spent reactor fuel and six canisters containing electric heaters,

207
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:27,320
which will be emplaced in the 17 dry wells drilled in the floor of a granite tunnel 1,400 feet beneath the surface.

208
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:39,680
Each spent fuel canister is taken to the experimental facility by a specially designed surface transport vehicle which consists of a large steel cask mounted on a low boy trailer.

209
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:47,680
The cask is loaded in the E-MAD Hot Bay and then driven to the top of a vertical hole which connects the surface to the granite tunnel.

210
00:19:57,780 --> 00:20:06,480
Men and equipment are transported to the 1,400-foot deep tunnel in a separate vertical shaft.

211
00:20:06,580 --> 00:20:11,039
The canister containing the spent fuel assembly is carefully lowered underground through the

212
00:20:11,139 --> 00:20:16,180
19-inch diameter access hole to a rail-mounted transfer vehicle.

213
00:20:16,280 --> 00:20:20,880
All of this equipment is controlled from the surface with the operations being monitored

214
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:22,620
on closed circuit television.

215
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:30,620
When the underground transport vehicle has received the canister, it moves to one of the storage holes drilled on the floor of the tunnel.

216
00:20:31,720 --> 00:20:35,620
A 5,200 pound concrete cover over the hole is removed

217
00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:39,620
and the canister is lowered into place along with a shield plug.

218
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:47,620
A specially trained team then connects temperature and radiation monitoring instruments in the hole

219
00:20:47,720 --> 00:20:50,620
and the concrete plug is replaced, covering the hole.

220
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:59,980
Two other tunnels in the granite formation on either side of the fuel emplacement tunnel

221
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:04,180
have been equipped with electric heaters and monitoring equipment.

222
00:21:04,280 --> 00:21:10,280
The electrical simulators will be controlled to match the heat output of a spent fuel assembly.

223
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:15,200
The object of the experiment is to evaluate the response of granite to the temperatures

224
00:21:15,300 --> 00:21:21,239
produced by the electric heaters and the combination of high radiation and thermal heat produced

225
00:21:21,339 --> 00:21:24,920
by the radioactive spent fuel assemblies.

226
00:21:25,020 --> 00:21:29,480
This data will aid in determining whether or not granite and other crystalline rock

227
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:35,099
types are suitable geologic formations for permanent disposal of high level radioactive

228
00:21:35,199 --> 00:21:37,019
wastes.

229
00:21:37,119 --> 00:21:42,160
All of the spent fuel canisters are retrievable and will be moved from the various experimental

230
00:21:42,260 --> 00:21:46,420
locations back to the EMAD building after several years.

231
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:51,180
The spent fuel assemblies and the dry wells will then be examined to determine if there

232
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:57,739
has been any effect caused by dry storage in an inert atmosphere.

233
00:21:57,839 --> 00:22:02,820
The information gained from these experimental programs will be used to develop geologic

234
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:09,920
repository technology for use in permanent geologic waste disposal programs.

235
00:22:10,020 --> 00:22:15,239
A third study on high-level waste management involves determining if a geologic formation

236
00:22:15,339 --> 00:22:20,239
suitable for permanent disposal of high-level waste exists within the boundaries of the

237
00:22:20,339 --> 00:22:23,640
Nevada test site.

238
00:22:23,740 --> 00:22:28,599
Any long-term waste management project must be compatible with the prime test site mission

239
00:22:28,699 --> 00:22:31,680
of underground nuclear weapons testing.

240
00:22:31,780 --> 00:22:36,339
A waste repository located in the southwest quadrant of the site would not interfere with

241
00:22:36,439 --> 00:22:41,739
other Nevada test site programs and the search is centered on Yucca Mountain near the test

242
00:22:41,839 --> 00:22:44,480
site border northwest of the EMAD facility.

243
00:22:45,339 --> 00:22:52,480
The rock under evaluation at the site is welded tuff, a very hard volcanic formation located

244
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:56,880
about 2,000 feet beneath the surface.

245
00:22:56,980 --> 00:23:05,799
Currently, the area is being drilled to obtain cores of the underground rock formation for

246
00:23:05,899 --> 00:23:17,039
evaluation by geologists and other scientists.

247
00:23:17,139 --> 00:23:23,519
Other studies being conducted include surface ecology, seismicity, volcanism, and hydrology

248
00:23:23,619 --> 00:23:27,220
of the area.

249
00:23:27,320 --> 00:23:32,339
The effects of ground motion caused by underground nuclear tests are also being studied.

250
00:23:32,440 --> 00:23:37,820
If a geologic formation is found to be suitable for a permanent commercial high-level waste

251
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:44,100
repository, any facility constructed would be licensed by the Nuclear Regulatory Commission

252
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:49,020
with consultation and concurrence with the State of Nevada.

253
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:55,120
All aspects of these investigations are periodically reviewed by scientific panels whose membership

254
00:23:55,220 --> 00:24:00,320
represents both the Department of Energy and independent scientific organizations.

255
00:24:00,419 --> 00:24:06,279
Details and results of these studies are available in open scientific literature and are presented

256
00:24:06,379 --> 00:24:15,939
to state officials and the public.

257
00:24:16,039 --> 00:24:20,179
The radioactive waste management programs conducted at the Department of Energy's Nevada

258
00:24:20,279 --> 00:24:26,059
test site are essential to many of the DOE's defense programs and are helping to solve

259
00:24:26,159 --> 00:24:31,220
the problems of permanent geologic storage of high-level waste from the nation's commercial

260
00:24:31,320 --> 00:24:32,899
nuclear power industry.
